Well, yes, but the posting interface of what you are
reading is broken if it folds URLs in the process of
posting them. Until W3C faces the need for a URI
folding indicator reserved character, URIs need to
be on a single line, full stop.

> It is, by the way, possible to strip a lot of
> redundant and unnecessary information from a
> google groups URI, in order to produce a short
> one-click link.

You don't even have to do that. if you're building a
link for others to use, open the Google Groups
"Individual Article" display, and capture _its_ URL
for publication; it's plenty short enough.

You're perhaps thinking of the Google Search URLs,
which are indeed a mess worth triming carefully
of all null content sub-specifiers before passing
those URLs along to others.

> I rather like Message-IDs, though, since I can use
> them to fetch the post into my newsreader if the
> server still has it, and find it in google with as
> much as three clicks if it doesn't.

Well, a couple of comments on that. First, message
IDs aren't necessarily "short". That I have seen,
there's no particular standard limiting their
length, and so some of them encode a fairly huge
amount of information encrypted in a way only the
ISP can invert [perhaps suitable for tracing an
article to its author], and end up even longer than
usual Google Groups article URLs.

Others are just of the form

     [historical site article sequence number]
                        [at]
                     [site.tag]

and those are fairly short.

Second, I've seen message IDs fail as a way to find
an article, in the Google Groups advanced search
[but I have no way to know if that is just typical
Google Groups search software brain damage [Google
Groups archive search is hideously broken as of
about ten weeks ago], or if the message ID is
sometimes really getting lost in the process of
archiving the message].

They are in any case a pretty _expensive_ way to
find an article, since they pretty much require that
either a full news-spool search be done, or a hash
of message IDs be made and maintained by the news
spool, which may well be exactly what is done.

I've also used message IDs for years and had them
work just fine, but that doesn't seem to be as true
today as it was in the past.

